going actually 30 years 38 years ago  maybe to 1981 when you were taking a  portrait session with a special person  named Toni Morrison can you take us back  to that day what do you remember Toni  Morrison walked into my studio in  February 1981 I was 29 I was sort of  starting my career as a photographer she  was smoking a pipe and I remember you  know someone asked me recently what do  you remember from that I remember her  confidence and as a photographer you  kind of read people and you can sense if  they're nervous and she did she was  perfectly fine and if she was cool and  that was the beginning of our friendship  it continued I became kind of  photographer of choice for her we did  book jackets together and press pictures  and later she inspired a whole series I  did starting with the blacklist on  identity and was really I think a  privilege just to be her friend of all  this time just used photographed when do  you realize that Toni Morrison needed a  documentary I think around the time of  the blacklist when we did I did a there  were 23 people in that film and I  thought each one of them deserved a  documentary but if I were to do the  first one it would be Toni Morrison and  you know at a certain point a few years  ago I just felt she was in her early 80s  and I thought it's now or never if I'm  really gonna do this  and Toni is a very private person she  does not authorize biographies she  bowknot she won't write an autobiography  and I asked her if she would consider it  and she did not say no and I knew that  was a yes and and it took a little time  to kind of figure out how I was going to  do this but you know she's 88 now can  you analyze for us a little bit this  very unique structure even the way you  photograph Toni versus the other people  the way you employ our  work still images and archival footage  how did that all come to you into this  amazing piece I was an art history major  at Columbia so I have a lot of love for  the art world and I knew a lot of the  artists I'd spent 20 years photographing  artists and I wanted to incorporate that  somehow into the film it's you don't  really see fine art paintings in  documentaries usually if you do this an  illustration of something but here I  wanted to kind of have it feel something  and it was also a way to bring in 24  african-american artists work into it  people who revered Tony loved Tony were  in you know very influenced by Tony I  also shot Tony direct to camera which I  had the the list films were direct to  camera and I loved that but I thought  here maybe I could have Tony tell the  story directed camera and everyone else  talks off camera and it was a way to  make her the center of it she's telling  her story and they're sort of secondary  talking about her you it worked but if  you if it didn't you know you're stuck  with it so it was a kind of a risky  thing to do because you not seen Docs  that do that but I felt that it was a  way to kind of give her agency in a  sense that it was her telling the story  she wanted to tell very intimately  before this break came to you what was  your idea of Tony Morrison  that's a good question because it's got  such depth to it I really can't I can't  put that in a particular I can't  capitalize that because she is such  she's such a force and her awareness of  that kind of presence so she's so  authentic and that's the thing that  really continues to be very important  for me in resonates with me but when  Timothy called  and said  Toni Morrison that's all I needed to  hear you know I knew that it was going  to be an incredible and powerful journey  creatively and I will say this that they  Timothy and his team you all gave me  incredible flexibility to to try  different textures I mean this this  score has so many different genres that  I love jazz vocals solo piano fiddle  upright bass and a lot of it was music  that had been tempted in terms of  textures you guys had placed a lot of  upright bass and so that was a good  point of departure but we all just you  know it's it's her work has an energy  field that's very very powerful and very  visceral and I think that as a creative  person if you can surrender or yield to  that then the work itself begins to it's  conversation and it begins to gestate it  begins to create itself and in trusting  that and having a group of people that  are in that synergy is really what  helped to create this this score for me  this song that closes the film was  composed and performed by you thank you  thank you so much that was another I  mean this was like I just want to take  them with me wherever I go can you you  guys be my yeah clone that was an  incredible gift because typically you  know the in title it's it's a song that  you can they could have licensed it or  had others created but I think we had  all been in this really very very  intense and an intimate process so again  it was just it was a creative choice  that I really really appreciate this  film has to do a lot about race in  America  what is your reflection about the  importance of this film and Toni  Morrison's not just in the past but in  the present and what is the optimistic  view that you can give us for the future  I'm not sure I can give an optimistic  view of the future will know that in  2020 you know I think the themes that  Tony started out with that they're part  of her life are as important today as  they were then they're even they  resonate more and more she is a kind of  figure that we all follow that you know  her ideas and her sense of justice and  her sense of humanity are so powerful  and and I think also so inspirational I  mean it's not just to African Americans  I think this is a person who speaks to  people all over the world so it's a it's  just such a rare person Toni Morrison I  feel that the issue of race at this time  is so charged because of the systemic  recycling of a narrative that absolutely  has to be addressed but beyond that  narrative what what I like to call  attention to is the belief system that  creates the narrative in the first place  we never we never really go into that we  we have a tendency to get very consumed  by the constructs which are which are  absolutely present so the beauty the  beauty about Toni and that last scene in  particular is that she she brought it  all home in talking about the essence of  humanity which is that in the unknown  between us is more of the known and more  of the shared and so from me I I sort of  I look to life that way and music is in  and of itself it's such an otherworldly  force it's such you know when you're  right when I'm writing the process for  me is extremely organic it's very  present and so I don't have any  room for second guessing that because  it's so precious so that's how I'm  informed we would like to thank you very  much for being with us tonight
 

